On 22 May 1996, Tom Ivar Helbekkmo wrote:
> Actually, unless this is a very late version of Ultrix, it's possible
> to boot passwordless to single user mode to change the password.  It's
> been a while since I've used Ultrix on DECstations, so I don't recall
> the sequence of actions and commands needed, but I'm sure someone can
> supply these.

Here's a trick that should work:

1. Shut the system down and open the box.  
2. On the CPU module, just in front of the TURBOchannel slot nearest the
power supply are two blocks of jumpers; a set of four, probsably on
a red mount and a pair two.  This is also near where the board ways
"WSE".  Turn the power off, short the pair, turn the power on and
after a couple of seconds turn the power off.  This will clear the
CPU's NVRAM where the password is stored.  It will also clear the
boot path and boot mode.
3. The system will now boot to console "monitor" mode.
4. Reset your boot mode and boot path
5. Use the boot command to boot to single user mode.
6. Use passwd to change the root password.
7. Use the shutdown command to bring the system back up to multiuser mode.


