Kevin Fisher wrote:
> 
> Hello:
> 
> Recently I acquired a working Digital GIGI system, with cables and
> no manuals.  I've tried hooking the RGB outputs of the machine
> to my RGB monitor at home, but the monitor doesn't seem to be able to
> sync.

 
> I'm assuming that the resolution on this (circa 1981) machine is
> considerably lower than SVGA, so I'm wondering what kind of monitor
> is needed to work with this machine.  I have an old Amiga RGB monitor,
> but before I go to the trouble of making a cable for it I'd like to
> know if it's worth the effort beforehand.
>

The "standard" color monitor that was used with the GiGi (VK100) was 
made by Barco.  Sorry, I don't remember the resolution, sync rates, ect. 
but I doubt if they were anywhere close to SVGA.  
 
> And out of curiosity, does anyone know what kind of external storage
> devices this machine used?  (Not to mention, where on earth did it
> plug in to...the 20mA loop?!)
>

I don't believe there was any storage device that could be connected 
directly to the GiGi.  It was intended to either act as a graphics 
terminal, or have a Basic program downloaded to it from a host computer.
(or you could type in a basic program in local mode and run it, but 
it's rather tedious to type in anything but the simplest programs
every time you want to run one.)

The 20ma port was just one of the 2 available host connections.  You 
could select either the standard RS232 port or the 20ma port for host 
communication.
 
All of the above is from memory and may be partly or totally incorrect.
It's been a LONG time since I saw a GiGi.

