|perform an rlogin on anakin and check the size of the file "gsrs04973.bak",
|which I have just transfered to the UNIX disk from the VAX disk.  I find
|that the file is 24 bytes SHORTER on the UNIX disk than it was on the VAX 
|disk.  Note that, on the VAX, the file was 24 BLOCKS long.  Thus, it appears
|that one byte-per-block is being lost in the VAX -> UNIX transfer.  When
|I type the file on the VAX, I find that the file consists of 24 records, each
|consisting of 512 bytes, and each ending with a carrige-return character.

TYPE doesn't show you what's REALLY in the file.  If you look
at the file as such, you'll find that each record is really
514 bytes (2-byte prefix + 512 byte payload).  NO carriage 
returns at the end of each record.

|When I cat the file on ANAKIN, I find that these carriage-returns are MISSING.

Sounds like your FTP client (by the way, what TCP/IP package are
you running on the VMS side?) is, when you send this file as
TYPE IMAGE (i.e. "binary") sends just the record contents, not
the 2-byte prefixes (which you don't want anyway.)  It doesn't
send any carriage returns 'cause they're not in the file and
you didn't ask for TYPE ASCII anyway.

|	Do you know how I can properly transfer the entire file from VAX
|to UNIX?  Keep in mind that I must keep the carriage returns in the file.
|The program which reads the file requires the presence of the carriage
|returns.

I'm sensing some serious confusion here -

1. You don't really WANT the entire original file (2-byte record
   prefixes and all) to be transfered to Unix, do you?  Does your
   program on Unix know what to do with these record prefixes?

2. There are no carriage returns at the ends of the records in this 
   file on the VAX in the first place - DUMP it and see.

3. Unix doesn't even WANT to see any carriage returns at the ends
   of records anyway - it likes LINE FEEDs.

4. You didn't let us know what TCP/IP package you're using on VMS.

5. You didn't tell us what happened when you used TYPE ASCII, 
   which is the default.

My hunch is that, if you just use TYPE ASCII, the file will
transfer the way that you want it.  Or maybe you might have
to set the CR record attribute ( SET FILE /ATT=(RAT:CR) if
you're running V6.0 or better) on the file before FTPing it.

Here's a hint:

On the VMS side, DUMP tells you what's really on disk.  DUMP/RECORD
shows what's really in each record.  On the Unix side, od tells
you what's on disk (Unix has no concept of records.)  If you have
TCPDUMP (which comes with MultiNet and which is available for Unix),
you can watch the FTP data packets and ind out what's REALLY
going across the wire.

Figure out what your original file REALLY looks like, how you
REALLY want it to look on the Unix side, what is REALLY going
across the wire, and what TCP/IP (and OS) software you're actually
running, and we might be able to puzzle this out.

Cheers,

Aaron

