   That's a rather open-ended question.

   With a VT320 text terminal for a display, you are restricted to
   text-terminal-oriented displays.

   The current release of OpenVMS is V7.0.

   There are various Internet applications -- PERL5, mxrn, etc, available
   as part of the Digital TCP/IP Services (UCX) package, and/or as part
   of the OpenVMS Freeware CD-ROM and/or as part of DECwindows Motif
   (see DECW$UTIL:, as well as DECW$EXAMPLES:).

   The freeware CD-ROM locations, as well as various archive sites -- are
   in the FAQ -- see below. 

   Once you've got Lynx and/or Gopher installed and running, you can also
   use the Alta Vista or other search engines to locate information: see
   URL www.altavista.digital.com.

   You will  also need to obtain information specific to your site such as
   procedures for traversing any local firewall(s), any local site policies,
   etc.
 
   There are various other Internet Tools packages for OpenVMS available
   (or immanent) from Digital, from other vendors, and from public-domain
   or shareware folks/sites.
   
