Subject: Re: Headless VAXstation 4000/60
Date: 1996/05/20
Message-ID: <1996May20.102641.80527@cc.usu.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 155942536
references: <4nfauc$fap@newshub.atmnet.net> <4nfqe7$lvd@nntpd.lkg.dec.com> <4nip3l$2s6o@news.doit.wisc.edu>
organization: Utah State University
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


In article <4nip3l$2s6o@news.doit.wisc.edu>, jpstott@src.wisc.edu (John Stott) writes:
> I was not aware that you could use the workstation keyboard input with
> a terminal.

You can use both the keyboard and mouse inputs with terminals. There's a 15-pin
connector used to bring the video, keyboard, and mouse ports to the back of
the monitor. If you can sccrounge up a mating 15-pin connector, you can build
a cable to bring the ports to standard 25-pin connector. For the 400s, the
the relevant portion of the pinout is:

       One port  The other port
      /-------\ /-------\
           Rx     Rx
         Tx |     | Tx
       GND| |     | |GND
        | | |     | | |
        v v v     v v v
        o o o o o o o o
         o o o o o o o

You will need to add a $SET TERM/PERM/TYPE_AHEAD for each line to the system
startup file to allow logins on the lines. I may have Tx and Rx backwards, but
I don't think so (and it would only take a moment to check with a voltmeter).

You can also do this with 2000s and 3100s, but the connector pinout is
different (and escapes me at the moment). I've hung terminals off the keyboard
and mouse ports of a 2000, 3100/30, 4000/60, 4000/90, and 4000/96.
