In article <4n4mkm$4er@scotsman.ed.ac.uk>, ajmy@tattoo.ed.ac.uk (A Myles) writes...
>shoppa@erich.triumf.ca (Tim Shoppa, x6446) writes: 
>>Perhaps someone decided that area needed some WD40 ? Nothing is worse
>>for delicate electronic equipment than randomly spraying areas with WD40.
>>It's an excellent way of turning dust into gunk.
> 
>On Friday night I finally got around to craking open the 3200 and pulling
>out the tk50 for a clean up. Fortunately it just had dust in it, but much
>of the internals had greasy muck. Luckily the boards were quite clean,
>though I don't have any spare Q-bus slots it seems.
> 
>Any suggestions about what's best for cleaning greasy muck? IPA?

Yes, Isopropyl is the way I usually start.  Most plastics and rubber
components won't be adversely affected by short exposure to
isopropyl (though I hear it does have a tendency to dry out rubber
pinch-rollers).  Anhydrous or 99% isopropyl are the least likely to
have residues in them; "rubbing alcohol", I hear, actually has some
oils mixed into it.

The grease-removing kitchen
cleaners are also good, but are more likely to leave behind some residue.
And for the really thick goo (especially foam and adhesives), kerosene
works wonderfully.  Be sure to clean up any residue left behind by these
cleaners with isopropyl.  Also be aware that the stronger stuff is much
more likely to attack plastic or rubber parts, and that skin exposure
to kerosene and other strong organic solvents is not the best.  (Skin
exposure to anhydrous isopropyl isn't wonderful either, but at least it's
worst effect seems to be dry skin.)

If they're still available in your area, the various freon cleaners are
really quite excellent.  Many of the rubber parts on my 9-track tape drives
are marked "clean only with freon-xxx", where xxx is some particular
combination.  In California, it was pretty much impossible to buy these
cleaners - I'll have to see if they're available here in Canada or not.

