In article <01bb42b1.cd78a0e0$708466ce@office1.mtco.com>, Eric L. Maki <elmaki@mtco.com> writes:
|>I just grabbed a new DEC Alpha 1000/266 and I am getting ready to install
|>the Digital Unix that came with it.  Is there anyway I can increase the /
|>partition beyond the 67.1 Megs available.  for some reason, If I leave the
|>partitioning Scheme that way as soon as I perform a dump to tape, my /
|>parttion gets filled to 111%
|>
|>Is this just a bug, or is it something I can fix easily?
|>
|>Eric L. Maki
|>

It's relatively easy to fix.  Start the installation but instead of choosing
BASIC or ADVANCED, select SYSTEM MANAGEMENT.  At this point, you drop out of
the installation procedure and are left in single-user mode.  From here, you
can invoke disklabel (you may have to create the device file, i.e. cd /dev; 
/MAKEDEV rz0).  Using disklabel, you can adjust the size of the "a" and any
other partition.

Be careful when installing that you don't select BASIC.  This type of install
uses all defaults, including partition tables - so any customization you did
would be disregarded.

FYI - in V4.0, there is a graphical disk partitioning tool integrated into the
installation to make this a much simpler operation.

