In article <4n0dv5$1nq8@mule2.mindspring.com>, tom@automatedtech.com (Tom Hickerson) writes:

:The system is an AXP 150 which is still under warranty. 
:DEC "Carry-In" service is working on it now. 

   Ok, I'm going to drop out of the thread, then, as the service
   folks should be able to localize this.

:The problem occurs under both VMS and NT running
:on different disk drives.

   The amount of heat generated by a disk drive varies widely by the
   type/model of disk drive and the disk drive mounting -- *this* is
   why folks are asking for specific details of the hardware
   configuration.

:                           ...   The symptom is
:that all system activity appears to slow, 
:processes begin to timeout, and then it hangs.

   If it were occuring on one or the other, I might say it were an
   operating system problem -- with both operating systems affected,
   it's probably hardware.  (Given one switches PALcode for NT and
   OpenVMS, it does not appear to be PALcode.)

:No traps or machine checks.

   Ok.  Any errors?

:Opening up the box and blowing air into it with
:an external fan appears to solve the problem.
:Internal fans appear to be operating.

   Are the air vents blocked in some fashion?  Are the internal fans
   turning at full speed, or are they spinning over at idle?

   And this is why I was asking about the SIMMs -- there are any number
   of components that can be affected by heat, and SIMMs are a common
   user-inserted system component, and some SIMMs -- bluntly -- stink.
   (I've seen a number of "overrated" SIMMs, and heat-sensitive SIMMs.
   These SIMMs work fine on low-end systems, but Alpha systems _need_
   the SIMMs to run at the rated speed.)
   
:>  Seriously, you might want to provide the system hardware type, the
:>  current hardware configuration including the current mass storage
:>  configuration, memory SIMM type, the operating system and version,
:>  and a, uh, slightly less terse problem description. 
:
:>  The ambient temperature (if unusual) and whether or not the fans are
:>  spinning might also be interesting.
:
