In article <1996May12.075724.15835@decus.org.au>, manson@decus.org.au writes:
>In article <1996May9.094719@cim.alcatel.oz.au>, quodlingp@cim.alcatel.oz.au writes:
>> ...
>> This product is CDD, which we use fairly extensively. However most of the
>> projects using it are fairly dated, and we are actually running CDD V4.2-1,
>> which is prior to the use of RDB RT to manage the CDD repository. Our RDB RT
>> Version is at 3.3-1, and to muddy the waters we also have datatrieve V5.1 which
>> needs to be synchronized with all of this.

CDD/Plus V4.0 was the first version of CDD to use Rdb.  The name was changed
to CDD/Repo in V5.0 when it got a bunch of useless extensions that made it
even more complex and incomprehensible to its developers than V4.*.  If you
are running CDD V4.2-1, it uses Rdb for its CDO databases.

If you are using any CDO dictionaries, you have to upgrade them to Rdb V6.1.
Check Rdb's and CDD's release notes for how to do it.  Once you have your
dictionaries' CDD$DATABASEs upgraded to a current Rdb, you can
$ @SYS$LIBRARY:CDD$UPGRADE them.  If you're going to upgrade, I suggest 
going to both Rdb and CDD V6.1.  CDD V6.1 finally has shaken out many of
V4.0's and V5.0's bugs and is nowhere near as bad a product as its 
predecessors.  Rdb, on the other hand, has been an outstanding product and 
just keeps getting better.  I can't imagine any reason to use an older version
of Rdb if you're going to go through the trouble of upgrading.

If you are only using DMU dictionaries, there is no upgrade necessary.
DMU dictionaries have not changed since the last V3.? release.

>Anyway, I still haven't had a reasonable answer about the on-going "life"
>of DTR, or CDD for that matter.
>
>I assume from your details, that your site, like mine, uses CDD and DTR,
>but not RDB. I understand that CDD was part of the sell-off package with
>RDB, but DTR wasn't.  We would happily live without CDD, but DTR doesn't
>like that.

The latest or impending release of DTR still supports the ancient
DMU dictionaries, modern CDO (Rdb-based) dictionaries, and has added 
support for its own dictionaries.  You can throw away CDD and use DTR's
own private dictionary.  See DTR's release notes for the minimum 
required version of CDD if you use it, or forget CDD.  You can extract 
all of your dictionaries' elements into DTR syntax (DTR> EXTRACT ALL 
command) from your current DTR, then remove CDD, upgrade DTR, and
reload all the extracted definitions into DTR's new non-Oracle dictionary.

You should be able to massage DTR syntax record definitions into SDL if
you want other languages to be able to read them.  Once translated to SDL,
you can keep the master record definitions in SDL and let the now free
SDL (see the VMS Freeware CD) translate them to all of your favorite 
