>Hopefully I've put these two boards back in the right order (board 1
>nearest the fans); it was necessary to remove them both at the same
>time, as a washer fell down underneath the card-edge connectors, which
>I had to remove as well to get to the washer.  You needn't worry about
>me twiddling pots at random - I want to restore this system, not
>destroy it.  :-)

I would still recomend testing the PSU on a dummy load before you 
reconnect the CPU to it. You may have done something silly, and you don't 
want to wipe out every chip in the system...

As you've discovered (I hope), restoring one of these machines is a long 
job. You're for ever doing a little work, checking it, checking it again, 
and then going on to the next bit. But it's worth it in the end. 


>>Do you have the printset? There's some info in there, I think, and more 
>>in volume 1 of the maintenance manual.

>Unfortunately, the answer is no.

Pity. They're very useful documents. Look out for a set - they do turn up 
from time to time, either printed or on microfiche. The PDP8/e 
maintenance manual is 3 volumes. Volume 1 covers the CPU and standard 
options (4K core, TTY interface). Volume 2 covers the internal options 
(ROMs, EAE, etc), while volume 3 covers the external options (printer 
ports, disk and tape controllers, etc). They give simplified schematic 
sections, theory, and test information. The printsets are, of course, the 
full schematics and layouts for the machine.



