In article <4mo0dp$6ic@explorer.csc.com>, cagard@csc.com (Cynthia Agard) writes:
:This old fortran vax/vms programmer is having problems with decc alpha 6.1
:environment.

   I'll assume OpenVMS Alpha V6.1 based on the version number.

   Are you familiar with the OpenVMS source code debugger? 

:Tell me what is wrong with this??
:I'm skipping over stuff and showing you the meat of my problem.

   It's not particularly obvious from the code that was posted.

:The following is contained in a single module which blows up whenever
:I run it.

   The contents of the access violation message typically point to
   some interesting information relevent to the fault -- you already
   appear to know the PC, but it also points to the address that was
   referenced when the access violation occured, and whether or not
   the access was a read or a write.  "$ HELP/MESSAGE ACCVIO" will
   get you some information on what I mean here...

   Use the source code debugger, and step through to the failing source
   line.  (SET BREAK/EXCEPTION; GO is one way to get to the failing line
   quickly.)  Then see what's wrong with the code near the area:

: /* sure am skipping over a bunch of stuff */
: suffix_loc = strlen (attrname) - 1;
: attrname [suffix_loc]++;   /*=====THIS IS WHERE IT EXPLODES ACC VIO */ 

   You'll want to see if suffix_loc is valid, and you'll want to see
   if attrname is valid.  Then you'll want to figure out how things
   got confused...

