John W. Manly wrote:
> 
> My question is, what is the main differences between the AlphaServer line
> (1000, 2000, 2100) and the AlphaStation line (200, 250, 400, 600) as far as
> why I might want to buy one or the other?  Are there vast differences in the
> I/O bandwidth of the units (since I believe the processors are the same,
> varying in generation and clock speed by the 4/233 and 5/300 monikers).
> The differences are not so much in processor speed and I/O bandwidth as in
robustness and serviceability.
The servers have ECC memory vs parity in the workstations. Hence a memory 
error will not crash the process or the system.
Apart from the 1000, the servers allow multiple cpu's, which can provide
more graceful behaviour under heavy load. (I've certainly seen this with
OpenVMS systems, I don't know how well SMP Windows NT systems behave.)
The servers have StorageWorks disks, allowing hot swap of defective drives
(you will still have other issues such as stopping SCSI bus traffic and 
keeping the op-sys happy.) The workstations typically have internal drives,
and you have to open the skins to change them.
Servers are available in rack-mount versions, which makes cable management
much easier for large configs.
The new low profile 2100A has 2 separate PCI buses, which does give it a
significent boost in I/O bandwidth.
I think the 4100 has 2 64-bit PCI buses, which will be even better, when 
there are some 64-bit PCI cards.
The AlphaStation 600 has many of the characteristics of a server, and could
be a good choice if you can get the right price.
