From: lueck@gurgi.zk3.dec.com (Greg Lueck Development Environment)
Subject: Re: loading data addresses on Alpha
Date: 1996/05/07
Message-ID: <4mnecs$j5f@lead.zk3.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 153510135
distribution: world
references: <Dqs8D7.7A3@world.std.com>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
reply-to: lueck@zk3.dec.com (Greg Lueck Development Environment)
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec



The compiler load addresses from a table of address literals.  The $gp
register is reserved to point at this table, so a typical address load
looks like this:

	ldq	$rx, disp($gp)

where "disp" is the appropriate displacement to reference the desired
element in the table.  The compilers also generate code to set up the
$gp register upon entry to a procedure.  You'll notice at the start of
each procedure there is code like this:

	ldah	$gp, disp1($pv)
	lda	$gp, disp2($gp)

This sets the $gp register by adding a 32-bit constant to the $pv
register.  The calling standard on Digital Unix Alpha dictates that $pv
be set to the address of the called procedure.  Therefore, there is a
limitation that the address literal table be within a 32-bit offset from
every procedure that uses it.

Since it sounds like you are in your own environment, you can either
choose to use this mechanism or choose another that is more appropriate
to your needs.  For instance, if address loads are uncommon, you can
avoid reserving a register and generate code like this to load an
address:

	br	$rx, 0   	; Branch to next instruction, $rx set to
				; address following branch
	ldah	$rx, disp1($rx)
	ldq	$rx, disp2($rx)

This loads an address from a table that is indexed by adding a 32-bit
displacement to the current PC.  Note, the whole sequence uses only one
register.

Another option is to load addresses like this:

	br	$rx, L1          	; $rx set to address following branch
	<low 32 bits of address>
	<high 32 bits of address>
    L1:	ldq	$rx, 0($rx)

However, this is rather messy because it mixes text and data together.
Also, you need to get the alignment of the branch instruction right or
the address load will be unaligned.

Hope this helps.

