fahad@cs.pitt.edu (Fahad A Hoymany) writes:

>Dave Olson (olson@anchor.engr.sgi.com) wrote:
>: That's a totally bogus statement.  Even Sun finally got it together
>: and does udp checksumming now.  Almost everybody else has been doing
>: it for ages.

>  UDP checksumming has always been optional (from the day UDP was
>invented), but it is useful to have it on for many (not all)
>applications. If Sun has finally started turning it on, that confirms the
>checksum is optional.  Also, look at Comer 1995 Volume I edition (page 182)
>which confirms this fact.


Dave was replying to your: <4ml74c$331@usenet.srv.cis.pitt.edu>
 
   
>No, they are hardly used and so generally they are not considered
>existent. It is optional.

And he's right that that's a bogus statement.  Modern implementations
use UDP checksums by default, and all implementations support them,
because the standard say they *must* implement them.

Sure, their use is optional; but the proper practive is using checksums.

(SunOS 4.x doesn't do checksums by default, it is recommended by many
that you turn them on, especially on DNS servers; When checksums are
turned off, SuNOS 4 also doesn't check incoming packets which checksums,
which isn't legal.  Checksums that are present *must* be checked)

Casper

