tom@dogbert.de.sc.ti.com (Tom Sorensen) writes:

>TCP does not segment either. It's left up to IP and your MTU setting. Various
>TCP applications DO segment, often into 512 byte packets.

No. The segmenting of user data is one of TCP's prime functions. The
application does NOT segment; the network connection appears to the
application to be a stream of bytes with no record boundaries at all.
One of TCP's jobs is to decide how best to chop that up into segments
which are then given to IP for delivery. "Deciding" involves lots of
things, like the interface MTU, the 536-byte minimum MSS of the old
ARPAnet, the result of the path MTU discovery algorithm, the Nagle
algorithm, silly window avoidance, the PSH bit, and so on.


