In article <318C1E62.62A5@aol.com>, Joey Acampora <aquafreshq@aol.com> writes:

:I knew my wires were pinned correctly, and I was able to get the VT320 
:to function with a modem.  However, the connections were usually 
:unreliable (ie garbage and losing CARRIER).

   That usually points to a problem on the modems on either end, or
   (more likely) to a problem somewhere in the telephone circuit. 
   (While it could be a problem on the serial line, it'd take a fair
   amount of EMI to mess up a (short) MMJ wire run sufficiently badly
   for it to generate garbage characters on the wire, and MMJ cable
   faults are not usually this intermittant...)

   Runs of similar (garbage) characters typically point to T1 clock
   slippage, while random characters usually indicate line noise
   problems.
   
:A tech at DEC told me that the mmj pin-outs on the VT320 weren't well 
:suited for connecting to a modem (voltage too low or something).  He 
:recommended using a version of the VT320 with a db25 port on it (which I 
:luckily had).  No problems since.

   The MMJ uses DEC423 signalling (RS423), not RS232.

   Digital sells adapters that convert MMJ to DB25 RS232, and I've not
   seen any problems when using these adapters.  (These adapters are
   likely also available from third parties.)

   Note that some iterations of the RS232 standard are (were) rated at
   under 10 meters -- RS232 driver chips tend to be rather overdesigned
   and can push signals futher, but that's not a requirement.

   MMJ pinouts and adapter part numbers are listed in the OpenVMS FAQ
   (Frequently Asked Questions) posting.  MMJ does not have full modem
   control support -- there are not enough wires.

  ------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
