In article <1996May6.154224.891@flying-disk.com>, frisbie@flying-disk.com 
(Alan Frisbie) writes:
|>I am confused.   I am looking at the articles in DEC's
|>"Customer Update" on the AlphaServer 1000 and 1000A.
|>The first (1000) is in the November 1995 (Vol. 6, #11) issue
|>and the second (1000A) is in the February 1996 (Vol. 7, #2)
|>issue.
|>
|>On the second page of each article is a list of features.
|>What confuses me is the following:
|>
|>   AlphaServer 1000                   AlphaServer 1000A
|>        4/266                              4/266
|>   ================                   =================
|>
|>341 Transactions per SECOND        1150 Transactions per MINUTE
|>
|>There is no explaination of why there is such a drastic
|>difference between two almost identical machines, in articles
|>run only three months apart.

I forwarded your question to John Henning, who understands such things,
and he asked me to post the following response on his behalf - Steve.



These figures represented two different workloads, one of which was
measured in tps and the other in tpm.  The latter workload is far more
complex than the former workload, and is not directly comparable.  In
particular you should not expect to see 60x more tpm than you do tps.

Over the last couple of years, the transactions-per-second workloads have
been retired.   So what you are probably seeing is the effect of changing to
the newer workloads.

Another item to be aware of is that Digital (and many other vendors) at
one time would do both of the following kinds of TP benchmarking.  Since
December, 1995, the rules have changed to only allow #1:

   1. Run a full workload with a totally compliant database and subject
      it to an external audit.  Publish it as "x tpmC @ $y/tpmC".

   2. Run an UNauditted workload of similar complexity in order to 
      estimate what #1 would have given.  Publish it as estimated
      transactions per minute.

Since only #1 is allowed today, you should not see any new brochures
published by computer vendors with "estimated tpm".  If you do see such
a brochure (from Digital or anyone else) you can politely remind them
that the rules of TPC forbid vendors to:

    (1)     Use estimated results that refer to the TPC or TPC workloads, or to
            compare them to TPC results.

    (2)     Use results, metrics, or terminology which are not based upon
            official TPC results, but which could be reasonably inferred to 
            refer to the TPC or TPC workloads, or to be comparable to TPC 
            results.

And by the way the correct result for the AlphaServer 1000 4/266 is
1153.70 tpmC @ $229/tpmC.

Hope this helps
    /John Henning
     CSD Performance Group
     Digital Equipment Corporation
     henning@zko.dec.com
     Speaking for myself, not Digital





