|FWIW, I often have trouble with VT220s this way; garbage characters on a short
|cable between the VT220 and (for example) a MicroVAX. It seems to be some
|problem with grounding in the power supply, but I've never bothered to sort
|it out (I haven't gone through all my VT220s yet, and I still have two
|perfectly good VT52s before I have to get desperate and spend time fixing
|VT220s). However, on the bad VT220s it seems I can draw a spark when frogging
|around the RS232 connector with a scope probe.

Interesting. I experienced this problem too. I was (at that time)
using a PC clone as a terminal for my MicroVAX II. I connected up an
RS232 cable, but results were somewhat erratic. I noticed that sparks
could be persauded to leap between the DB25 shell and the MicroVAX
chassis by messing with the plug. Ouch!

I finally solved this problem by connecting a bit of wire between the
VAX chassis and the case of the PC. Whatever potential difference
there was between them vanished, and connections were reliable.

I've only ever seen this problem with the MicroVAX II (not with my
11/23+, which uses the same BA23 box, or my 11/34a) so I guess it is a
wierdness in the VAX design. FWIW, I checked the earthing on the VAX
and found it to be OK.

Maybe the earthing in my house is plain dodgy ...


