X-Deja-AN: 153909424
sender: root@primenet.com
references: <4mlprd$4va@access1.digex.net> <4mmnq9$55q@access4.digex.net>
organization: Billy's Place
x-posted-by: billy@usr07.primenet.com
newsgroups: vmsnet.pdp-11,alt.sys.pdp11,comp.sys.dec


R. D. Davis <rdd@access4.digex.net> writes:

> There are no DD*.SLP files in with my (partially complete?) 6.01
> distribution; will I need them, or should the DD500 and DD501 .SLP
> files be all that I'll need?

TSX is distributed with a built DD.TSX handler.  If you have DD.MAC
from which ever RT-11 you're using to start TSX you can do this patch
manually - all it does is move a buffer to par 5.  The whole thing is
just a few lines..

> Am I going to need to define a line definition block to use the DL11
> line No. 0?

No, just set DD's CSR and vector to it.  Also be sure nothing else
is talking to the same place, no line def block, no LP, etc, etc.

> If I understand correctly, the next thing that I would need to do
> would be to create a file named TSXCND.MAC, containing the lines:

> MMG$T = 1
> ERL$G = 0
> TIM$IT = 1

You'll need this to build DD.TSX, yes.  It should also say -

  TSX$P = 1

This is for any TSX-Plus conditional assembly, such as the above
patch.

> Then, after doing that, build the DD.TSX device handler for DD - this
> looks simple enough, but don't I need a DD.MAC file in order to do
> this?  I seem to recall some [device].MAC files in the RT-11
> distribution (but I could be mistaken) - would this be what I'd use?

Yes, after patching it per the above.  You patch the .MAC source,
then build it.

> MACRO TSXCND+DD/OBJ
> LINK/EXE SY:DD.TSX DD

If you're using RT-11 V5 or above, add the /NOBITMAP switch to
the link command.

> Am I correct to think that the next, and final, step would be to make
> an entry in TSGEN using the DEVDEF macro (what's this?) for the device
> to be added?  Would I use TXMOD for this?

As long as there's a free device slot I believe TSXMOD can do this.
Run it, issue the SH DEVDEF command, then enter DEVDEF <DD>,... adding
which ever options you'd like in the same format as SH DEVDEF displays,
then EXIT writes the changes back to the TSX image.  You'll then have
to restart TSX to actually load DD.

Billy Y..



