A.R. Duell <ard12@eng.cam.ac.uk> wrote:
>rdd@access1.digex.net (R. D. Davis) writes:

>I will have to check the printset for you. If it's exactly 6 leads, it's 
>probably a double transistor. If it's more, it may be a 723 regulator chip.

Actually, there are two of these (slightly different) - both are, upon
closer examination, actually 10-pin devices.  

On Control Board A1, this is an FU5R, 7723393, 7115 (that's either a 5
or a 3).

On Control Board A2, it's a TUSK (RUSK? something else?) 7323393,
2135 (2125?).

It was difficult to read some of those numbers, as the writing is
fading away, or coming off the cans.

Also, the circuit board is turning brown underneath of the 2W
resistor, and the 1W resistor next to it.  Is this normal?  If so, why
didn't DEC space these about 1/8" or so above the circuit board?

One more question: what adjustments are made using the trimmer
pots. on these boards?

>No, more like about 12 were defective, and another 3 bad. I've not got 

It's amazing that one should find 12 defective fans, out of 18, in a
single machine.  What on earth happened to this machine to make them
go bad?  Was some of this due to open wiring, etc., or were they
mostly mechanical problems?  

>the machine running yet, but I'm wondering just how many of the (rather 
>expensive) chips will need replacing. 

Hopefully, none will need replacing.  This is what I don't like about
integrated circuits - they contain too much non-repairable circuitry,
and one can't just replace them by modifying the circuitry slightly to
accept similar alternate spare parts.  ICs should be more modular; one
single one shouldn't perform so many operations.  Microprocessor ICs,
for example, should be broken down into components, such separate ICs
for the ALU, registers, etc.  I suspect that part of the goal in the
design of many of the VLSI chips, etc. is actually planned
obsolescence, so as to force people to purchase new equipment every
few years.





