Rick Jones (raj@cup.hp.com) wrote:
: Larry McVoy (lm@neteng.engr.sgi.com) wrote:
: : If you are just trying to get to 35MB/sec, you can easily do that SGI to
: : SGI today using Hippi & TCP.  In fact, I'm just on the verge of sending
: : out an extension to NFS that lets unmodified (usually) applications get
: : those sorts of speeds (up to 65MB/sec for one file) over NFS.  The usually
: : means that the applications have to be doing large (like a MB or more)
: : aligned reads & writes.

: Is that read or write, and if write, v3 with async, or v2? From
: filesystem cache on the server, or disc? (I'm assuming into memory on
: the client?)

read or write.  It's a bypass, works with v3 or v2.  Write semantics are
sync, async isn't even an option in BDS.  All data is to/from disk, this
mode has no caching (caching doesn't make much sense at these I/O rates).
Into memory on the client.

: : In SGI's stack, we play tricks to make sure the overhead is minimal.
: : The processors never touch the data, checksum is done in hardware.

: What are the restrictions on the application buffer alignment? Are any

Page aligned, multiple of page size.

: of the third-party cards for SGI providing the checksum
: offload/header-data split functionality? (HP has similar issues)

Dunno.

: : So the overhead is just the packet processing.  Given that you can do
: : 48K plus header sized packets on Hippi, the packet processing is usually
: : in the noise.

: There's a good question - is the origial author trying to do those
: rates 32KB at a time, or with little 1024 byte messages? 

If the app is stupid enough to do 1K messages, it loses.  

