>I don't know about dust, but this was real gunk, i.e., dust+grease+dirt.  It
>appeared to be physically interfering with the motion of the head.

Perhaps someone decided that area needed some WD40 ? Nothing is worse
for delicate electronic equipment than randomly spraying areas with WD40.
It's an excellent way of turning dust into gunk.

Someone mentioned JEM computers in a TK-50 thread recently.  A cow orker
and I ordered 4 TK50's and 4 TK70's from them a few months ago, at $15
each.  All the TK70's appeared fine, and as a matter of fact appeared
to be sealed in DEC boxes with DEC stickers indicating they had been
recently refurbished.  All were quite clean, and worked fine.  (If
only TQK70 controllers were readily available...)

Of the 4 TK50's, only two worked out of the box.  The TK50's weren't
in nice custom-fitted cardboard boxes with DEC stickers, and some had
a fair amount of dust on the internal parts.  A third TK50 was
easily repaired by re-hooking the leader which had gone astray.  And
the 4th TK50 appeared to be failing its power-on self test, as all
it would ever do is blink the red light.

JEM offered a 90-day waranty on the TK drives, if I remember right,
but I decided not to return the nonfunctional TK50.  It would've
been too much hassle to pack up, and anyway, it had a good DEC
mounting sled on it, complete with the metal springs, that I had
other plans for anyway!  (It's been a while since I ordered the
sleds and springs from DECdirect, but if I remember right they
ran $13 or so for a set.)

I see that JEM is now selling "Defective TK50 tape drives" for
$2 each.  If those still have the sleds attached, it's a great
way to get such a sled for mounting drives in BA23/BA123 type
boxes.

JEM is on the web at http://www.tiac.net/biz/bargains, and they
can also be reached by phone at 1-800-536-2037 or 1-617-497-2500.
I'm not affiliated with them, but I am a well-satisfied customer.

