In article <4map6f$b2b@access1.digex.net>,
R. D. Davis <rdd@access1.digex.net> wrote:
> This is what I don't like about
>integrated circuits - they contain too much non-repairable circuitry,
>and one can't just replace them by modifying the circuitry slightly to
>accept similar alternate spare parts.  ICs should be more modular; one
>single one shouldn't perform so many operations.  Microprocessor ICs,
>for example, should be broken down into components, such separate ICs
>for the ALU, registers, etc.

Yup, such things did exist (AM2901 line of ALUs).  You don't find them
around much because they add a lot to the cost of manufacture.  If you
ever have to cost out a computer, you'll find that the cost is almost
directly proportional to the number of ICs in the machine.  The cost of
the ICs isn't usually as influencing a factor (unless you have a REALLY
expensive chip).  This is why manufacturers are always trying to reduce
chip count.  It is the most effective way to reduce costs.  A decrease
in cost of %5-10 can mean the difference between bankruptcy and a
thriving business!

>  I suspect that part of the goal in the
>design of many of the VLSI chips, etc. is actually planned
>obsolescence, so as to force people to purchase new equipment every
>few years.

I would be VERY surprised to find an IC manufacturer who's looking
for planned obsolescence.  Quite the contrary in fact.  ICs are
hard to design, incurring huge NRE (non recurring engineering) costs
in that design phase.  The manufacturers would just love to create a design
that they could sell forever...  unfortunately, their competitors won't let
them :-)

Cheers,
Kevin

