Dave Schneider <dschneid@auc.trw.com> wrote:

>I'm looking for data on how fast I can push data over a network using
>TCP/IP.  I need to push data from one host to another that is divided
>into 7 logical streams.  I would like to make each stream a TCP/IP
>socket so that my software will be portable to any network technology
>and I can develop it independent of the hardware.  My data streams
>have the following rates:

<SNIP>


In the case that the data isn't to be routed, you could get away with
using UDP (User Datagram Protocol).  It is used mostly for NFS.

It is much faster because it is not a "guarnteed" packet delivery
system.  That means, if packets get out of order (usually via the
internet or by using routers) they come in out of order.  Also, there
is no real checksum or any other types of error correction done.

I think the headers on UDP packets are something like 12 bytes.  It is
the simplest IP protocol and therefore the fastest.  Since it wasn't
meant for routing, such things as a TTL counter aren't bothered with
and don't take CPU speed or bandwidth away.

My tech descriptions might be a little off, but generally what I am
tring to say is that UDP is better for high speed point to point
connections and it is implemented in just about every IP system out
there.  Have fun!





