dunnett@mala.bc.ca (Malcolm Dunnett) wrote:
>
>   I have a question about memory configurations on the Alphaserver 1000A.
>
>   The SOC states that the memory kits include "four industry standard
>SIMMS and an ECC SIMM for ECC support". In the list of kits it appears
>that this "ECC SIMM" is just another SIMM of the same size ( eg the
>64MB kit is "5 x 16MB 70ns SIMMS"), is this correct or is there
>something "special" about the ECC SIMM?
>
>   What I'm trying to figure out is whether I can use standard
>70ns parity (or non parity) SIMMS to expand the memory in one of these
>systems or if I need to purchase Digitals memory kits ( which cost about
>2-3 times what anyone else would charge for the same amount of memory )
>
>    Thanks in advance.
>

The only difference is that the "ECC SIMM" is a depopulated SIMM in order to save cost on the
base system. A standard SIMM will work in the ECC SIMM location, in fact when a memory upgrade 
kit is orderred from Digital it only contains standard, fully populated, SIMMS so that the 
SIMMs can't be plugged in incorrectly. The ECC SIMM is only used in bank zero and is installed 
at the factory.

The standard disclaimers apply, we have had some problems with a few SIMM vendors and therefore 
do not ship their components.  You'll have to do you own testing.  We also do not recommend 
mixing SIMM speeds within a bank.

Nick Warchol
AlphaServer Engineering
Digital Equipment Corporation




