George Hau (gkth@mail.ast.cam.ac.uk) wrote:

: What exactly is a  PDP11/23?  (shows my age!).

It is the microprocessor version of the PDP-11 minicomputer.  The 23
differs from the previous LSI-11 micros in that it has memory mamagement
and a fairly complete implementation of the PDP-11 instruction set.
I too have fond memories of these things.  For years I breathed new
life into cast off ones that could no longer support the UNIX after
it became bloated and recycled them in to packet routers (not limitted
to the the LSI's either, I had 2 11/70's playing gateway as well, one
of which was for a period of time the busiest router on the Internet).

: Can the internal disk drives be used
: for PC's?

Chances are they are NOT SCSI (though we did have some early SCSI
periphrals on ours) and as such they would be difficult to interface.
Not really worth it when gigabyte drives are selling at the local
electronics place for under $300.

-Ron




