I remember in VMS development at one time there was a fastboot project.
(As some of you may know, a VMS system can take quite a while to boot 
depending on the startup scripts that a system admin has set up.)
The idea depended on taking a snapshot of the running system that would 
allow fastboot to reconstruct everything in a hurry.

The same idea was implemented in TOPS10 years before.  Called "system 
sleep".

It all works pretty well if you haven't changed your system configuration 
in the meantime.  Part of the reason systems boot slow is that they have 
to detect what devices are connected, and that usually involves timeouts 
to make sure the device isn't going to respond.

