Old-Fashioned Staffordshire Plate... wrote:
> 
> In article <4l2e5n$umu@usenet.pa.dec.com>, neideck@nestvx.enet.dec.com (Burkhard Neidecker-Lutz) writes...
> >>So much for this (STREAMS) being a main memory bandwidth benchmark!
> >>
> >Is your problem understanding why more CPUs give better performance ?
> >These are multiprocessors with pended buses, so more CPUs mean more
> >simultaneous outstanding bus transactions and hence more memory traffic.
> 
> I can see how it might be related to, as you say, 'usable' memory bandwidth
> but have some questions. Why does it go up almost linearly with number of
> CPUs?  Couldn't one cpu still queue multiple outstanding transactions?

Multiple outstanding transactions to main memory are relatively new.  As
near as I can tell, the R10000 is the only shipping CPU that supports them,
though the PA-8000 will as well.  If one looks at the number of bytes that
can be outstanding in requests to memory without stalling the cpu, the
numbers I have (and please follow-up with corrections if you have them) are:

R10000		512 bytes
PA-8000		320 bytes
21164		 64 bytes
PPC620		 64 bytes
UltraSparc-1	 32 bytes

All of which are, of course, below what one would see for a Cray vector
machine (with 1024 bytes per pending vector load), but the trend is there.
It will be interesting to see to what extent (if at all) the STREAMS 
benchmark correlates with these values.

