Warning: I'm going to sound like a salescritter for SGI.  People who
know me, know that I am not company centric, I'll nail SGI when we screw
up and praise Sun when they do something cool.  That said, this is an
area where SGI rules, dude.  We move bits like nobody else in the world.
And I mean nobody else.   Read on for details.


Dave Schneider (dschneid@auc.trw.com) wrote:
: I'm looking for data on how fast I can push data over a network using
: TCP/IP.  I need to push data from one host to another that is divided
: into 7 logical streams.  I would like to make each stream a TCP/IP
: socket so that my software will be portable to any network technology
: and I can develop it independent of the hardware.  My data streams
: have the following rates:

:       ------------                              ------------
:       |          |                              |          |
:       |          |------> 7.7 Mbyte/s --------->|          |
:       |          |------> 4.0 Mbyte/s ----------|          |
:       |          |------> 3.3 Mbyte/s --------->|          |
:       |  Host A  |------> 650 kbyte/s --------->|  Host B  |
:       |          |------> 650 kbyte/s --------->|          |
:       |          |------> 650 kbyte/s --------->|          |
:       |          |------> 650 kbyte/s --------->|          |
:       |          |                              |          |
:       ------------                              ------------
:                       Total = 17.6 Mbyte/s

If you are just trying to get to 35MB/sec, you can easily do that SGI to
SGI today using Hippi & TCP.  In fact, I'm just on the verge of sending
out an extension to NFS that lets unmodified (usually) applications get
those sorts of speeds (up to 65MB/sec for one file) over NFS.  The usually
means that the applications have to be doing large (like a MB or more)
aligned reads & writes.

If you have to maintain 7 streams, your safest bet right now is 7 FDDI
rings (probably not your cheapest bet).  You might want to start thinking
about 100Mbit ethernet as well, it's cheaper in general.

:     o  Amount of overhead added by TCP/IP

In SGI's stack, we play tricks to make sure the overhead is minimal.
The processors never touch the data, checksum is done in hardware.
So the overhead is just the packet processing.  Given that you can do
48K plus header sized packets on Hippi, the packet processing is usually
in the noise.

:     o  Latency added by TCP/IP

There's a different question.  We've optimized for bandwidth, not latency.
Round trip latency over SGI's Hippi/TCP implementation is typically around
1.2 milliseconds for a small packet.  Which is pretty long, considering
that we have a Hippi "fastpath" that we use for MPI that gets something
like 200-300 microseconds round trip.  But the fastpath has to map buffers
into your address space, and you have to busy wait, etc, etc.

:     o  Impact to processor loading to handle protocol stack

One processor can source or sink a Hippi stream.

:     o  What network hardware was used

Hippi.

:     o  If not possible today, when and what is possible today

He he he.  Anything is possible, how much money ya got?

: Any information or references to data regarding this problem would be 
: greatly appreciated (particularly actual test results). I will summarize
: the information that I receive and provide it to those that are
: interested.

See also http://reality.sgi.com/lm and snarf up the Usenix paper.
It talks about this sort of thing and breaks costs down into parts.
Note that the machines in that paper are all old, I don't think I've
updated it to include R10K numbers (which are faster, obviously).




