In article <318796CF.2781@neu.sgi.com>, "Kevin D. Kissell" <kevink@neu.sgi.com> writes:
>
> Multiple outstanding transactions to main memory are relatively new.  As
> near as I can tell, the R10000 is the only shipping CPU that supports them,
> though the PA-8000 will as well.  If one looks at the number of bytes that
> can be outstanding in requests to memory without stalling the cpu, the
> numbers I have (and please follow-up with corrections if you have them) are:
> 
> R10000		512 bytes
> PA-8000		320 bytes
> 21164		 64 bytes
> PPC620		 64 bytes
> UltraSparc-1	 32 bytes
> 

UltraSparc-1 can have 1 64-byte read and 2 64-byte writes to main memory 
outstanding simultaneously, along with a 64-byte cache to cache transfer.

In addition, the system interface of UltraSparc-1, called UPA, is designed as two 
independently operating channels, which we call classes. All cache misses are 
issued from one class, and all writes including writebacks and some other 
transactions are issued from the other class. Thus pending writes do not
block cache fills. Furtheremore, writebacks of dirty line, cache fill requests, and
copyback/invalidate of a dirty cache line when another cache wants it, 
can all occur simultaneously without blocking/interfering with each other (modulo 
coherence related serialization and shared resource contention in the system).
To maximize system performance, Ultrasparc-1 also has split transaction address and 
data busses and both are operated in parallel.
And the cache to cache transfer latency is of the same order (within a couple of 
system clocks) as cache to memory transfer latency.

Ultrasparc-1 can have up to a maximum of 11 transactions outstanding to the system 
simultaneously (as a combination of memory and I/O transactions), plus 1 copyback 
cache to cache transfer transaction issued to it from the system.
The following breakdown is for the max number of bytes that can be undergoing
transfer between ultrasparc-1 and the system concurrently:

read  1 * 64 bytes to memory
write 2 * 64 bytes to memory
copyback 1 * 64 bytes to another cache
write 8 * 16 bytes to i/o
Total=384 bytes.

FYI: The memory bandwidth on Ultra-1 (uniprocessor desktop) is 600 Mbytes/sec,
on Ultra-2 (dual processor desktop ) is 800 Mbytes/sec, and on the recently 
announced Sunfire servers 2.5 Gbytes/sec. 

BTW: all the above information is from memory and should not be construed as
definitive. Not speaking for Sun. 

Zahir



