"Kevin D. Kissell" <kevink@neu.sgi.com> wrote:
>
>Multiple outstanding transactions to main memory are relatively new.  As
>near as I can tell, the R10000 is the only shipping CPU that supports them,

I don't think so. UltraSPARC-1 supports multiple outstanding transactions
to memory (but only one outstanding read).

Perhaps you meant to say "multiple outstanding *read* transactions"?

>though the PA-8000 will as well.  If one looks at the number of bytes that
>can be outstanding in requests to memory without stalling the cpu, the
>numbers I have (and please follow-up with corrections if you have them) are:
>
>R10000		512 bytes

Does this number correspond to
a) 4 outstanding reads (e.g. cache misses) of 128-byte lines? or
b) N 128 byte reads and 4-N 128 writes, if so is N = 1, 2, or 3?
c) something else?

If the answer is a) or b), then I can't figure out how to fit this
piece of information with some other information available from
lmbench data on R10K  (see
http://reality.sgi.com/employees/lm_engr/lmbench/lmbench-summary)
where the memory read latency is given as 1115ns (I assume this is on a
Challenge?) and the memory bandwidths for read and bcopy are given as
101 MB/s and 59 MB/s. These bandwidths fit with the "one read
at a time" model (128 bytes / 1.115 microsec = 116 MB/sec, which is
in the ball park of the 101 MB/s measured by lmbench for reads).

If the latency is 1115ns and the CPU could indeed have reads for
up to 512 bytes outstanding then you ought to be able to see much higher
bandwidths than lmbench is reporting.

Or is it that the R10K supports more outstanding transactions than
the system it is currently shipping in (Challenge) allows from a single
CPU?

I thought the Challenge bus supports 8 outstanding transactions?
Do they each have to be from different CPUs?

- Ashok Singhal
Sun Microsystems




