Dave Schneider (dschneid@auc.trw.com) wrote:

: I'm looking for data on how fast I can push data over a network using
: TCP/IP.  I need to push data from one host to another that is divided
: into 7 logical streams.  I would like to make each stream a TCP/IP
: socket so that my software will be portable to any network technology
: and I can develop it independent of the hardware.  My data streams
: have the following rates:
: 
:       ------------                              ------------
:       |          |                              |          |
:       |          |------> 7.7 Mbyte/s --------->|          |
:       |          |------> 4.0 Mbyte/s ----------|          |
:       |          |------> 3.3 Mbyte/s --------->|          |
:       |  Host A  |------> 650 kbyte/s --------->|  Host B  |
:       |          |------> 650 kbyte/s --------->|          |
:       |          |------> 650 kbyte/s --------->|          |
:       |          |------> 650 kbyte/s --------->|          |
:       |          |                              |          |
:       ------------                              ------------
:                       Total = 17.6 Mbyte/s
: 
: 
: Host A is known to be an SGI Challenge while Host B has not been

You've got some serious transport bandwidth requirements!!  Actual 
TCP stack bandwith is likely to be 20Mb/sec.  You'll be eating 
several entire CPUs in an N CPU SMP box, for both A and B.

Another technology that is better suited to this type problem 
and bandwidths is:

- Clustered Boxes with shared memory technologies.

  DEC has Memory Channel amoung 4 boxes, that delivers honest
  35 Mb/sec transport, 5 usec latency.  Each box can be a huge and
  blazing fast SMP Alpha box.  More SpecInts/SpecFlp than any other
  vendor.

  Also, IBM/RS6000, and even Sun have some Clustered box and
  high speed shared memory technologies that span boxes.

There's much advantage of not pushing this amount of data over
a protocol stack!  The Clustered Boxes memory technology is similar
to DMA to-from RAM in each box.  Low kernel and application over head.

The main potential disadvantage for you, is that Box A and B have
to be within 32' of each other for DEC.  Other vendors may have 
other technologies that allow farther spacing of A and B if this
is a need.

Keep us informed of what you plan on doing?


Curt Smith
curts@compgen.com




