summary: Wheeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeee!!!


In article <4m5f01$4va@access4.digex.net>,
R. D. Davis <rdd@access4.digex.net> wrote:
>In article <4m4pll$1ui@lyra.csx.cam.ac.uk>,
>A.R. Duell <ard12@eng.cam.ac.uk> wrote:

>>Yes. Ball bearings need grease, while sleeve bearings need a light machine
>>oil. In a lot of cases, the sleeve bearings will soak up oil to some extent,
>>and leaving them in a bath of oil overnight is a good idea.
>
>I'll do that, as when I oiled it, it still doesn't spin anywhere near
>as freely as the other fan does.  

After letting the 3-in-1 oil soak in good, and applying some more,
wheeeeeee!!!  Now this fan doesn't seem to know when to stop spinning
after a gentle push on the blades! :-)  I may take the other fan apart
as well, and lubricate it, so that it will spin this freely.

There was one minor scare, however, when the circlip (or internal
retaining ring) that holds the top metal disk in place popped off
during reassembly.  After not being able to find it, I figured that I
was going to be in a bit of a temporary pickle, as it was my guess
that it had popped off into some dark and dusty corner of the basement
where only the spiders and an occasional mouse dare to venture.  

After telephoning nearby hardware stores, I discovered that not one of
them had any of these circlips in stock; a few didn't even know what a
circlip, or internal retaining ring, was.  A clerk at one so-called
hardware store indicated that I was asking for somthing obscene, for
some odd reason - there certainly are some peculiar people out there.
As a last resort, I rang a Home Depot store, which transferred my call
to the hardware department, which transferred the call to someone in
the plumbing department - when I called back a few hours later, a
clerk demanded to know what the retaining ring was being used for, and
then tried to transfer me to the electrical department since this was
a part used in a fan.  I managed to get through to someone in the
hardware department again who knew what these clips were, and was told
that they were out of stock for a few days.  Another search tonight,
with a flashlight, led to the circlip being found.

>Hopefully I haven't damaged anything by attempting to remove them -
>the metal used on the bottom end of the bearing assembly appears to be
>somewhat soft.  Aluminum, perhaps?  I may have slightly dented it
>(unless it wasn't perfectly flat to begin with) by trying to push the
>bearings out, by just lightly tapping on it with a hammer and metal
>rod.

Apparently nothing was damaged, as there's no noticeable binding, and,
this fan certainly spins quite freely.

